@Feux-de-joie The fate of *other people’s* natural resources is none of your concern.
Have you ever tried to calculate how many millions of indigenous people in the Americas died as a result of the actions of colonists? Or how many Africans—or, for that matter, people from India—perished? During the reign of the Belgian King Leopold II, the population of his colony, the Congo, was cut in half. In 1900, the life expectancy in that African nation was 30–32 years; today, it is over 60. Furthermore, the genocide orchestrated by the British in Bengal claimed the lives of approximately 3 million people.
Europeans continue to plunder these countries to this day, masking colonialism under the guise of "production-sharing agreements." In the grand scheme of things, nothing has changed. As Jacques Chirac once put it: "Without Africa, France would slide down to the rank of a third-world power."

@FortuneOfNations not only that. The rivers that Russia has are often too narrow, too shallow or constantly frozen. It was a blessing for defensive wars because the logistics would quickly become a nightmare for the enemy, but it is also a curse because the trade becomes rather expensive. And if the trade is expensive, then the progress slows down. Whatever discoveries Europe would make - it would arrive into Russia much later, and quite often through centralized effort from the government. The trade with Russia was rather unprofitable unless you trade directly with the Imperial Family's blessing. 

There was also an interesting case of Poland - when it was taken into the Empire, the Poles almost bankrupted Russian merchants, to the point where the Emperor had forbidden the Polish merchants from trading with the mainland Russia. 

I did study Asian history for like 6 years in university and I still cannot comprehend how it did survive. Those advantages that Russia used to survive quickly turned into disastrous disadvantages after the industrial revolution. Literally everything became flawed but, despite that, Russia continued to run the way it was familiar with, just like Qing China. It's like seeing a flying pig - you cannot explain it, but you also cannot deny that it is flying right before your eyes.
